Well, here's the thing.

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:23.974
I know what I would look for.

00:51:23.974 --> 00:51:24.870
That's one of the things you're doing

00:51:24.870 --> 00:51:25.970
as you watch the show.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:31.670
I firmly believe that while you could

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:36.670
get into some really like
analytical, it's a vibe.

00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:42.190
Hold on, I wanna say this is important

00:51:42.190 --> 00:51:44.310
because there are things like

00:51:45.860 --> 00:51:48.370
me and Jessie have talked about
this lately we've been like,

00:51:48.370 --> 00:51:50.000
you know how sometimes like,

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:55.000
you know someone and
you get a vibe from them

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:59.380
and you can be like, I think
this person is duplicitous.

00:51:59.380 --> 00:52:01.110
Or I think that this person,

00:52:01.110 --> 00:52:05.183
there's something off about
this person in their character.

00:52:07.291 --> 00:52:09.174
And you can kind of sense
it but then you're like,

00:52:09.174 --> 00:52:12.640
that feels judgemental, I'm
not basing that on anything

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:15.550
other than just a
conversation I had with them.

00:52:15.550 --> 00:52:17.537
And me and you have noticed this

00:52:17.537 --> 00:52:19.740
and there's been a
couple of times when like

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:22.787
we've met with someone in
like a business capacity

00:52:22.787 --> 00:52:26.032
and then we'll come out of
a meeting and we'll be like,

00:52:26.032 --> 00:52:28.790
I don't get a good vibe from that guy.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:31.183
And it might be that,

00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:34.440
but what I have learned is that

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:37.840
whatever is happening
when I say that to myself

00:52:37.840 --> 00:52:41.390
is more reliable than the
two hour conversation.

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:43.130
That me and you might have afterwards

00:52:43.130 --> 00:52:44.990
trying to rationalize our way

00:52:44.990 --> 00:52:48.340
into thinking whether or not
somebody is worth working with.

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:51.280
And that's what I think this
psychologist is picking up on

00:52:51.280 --> 00:52:55.710
is that the human capacity
for processing things

00:52:55.710 --> 00:52:58.760
in a way that you cannot even articulate

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:03.370
is way under appreciated by most people.

00:53:03.370 --> 00:53:06.940
In other words, the vibe
that you get from someone

00:53:06.940 --> 00:53:08.320
is very often reliable.

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:10.560
That doesn't mean you're
gonna be wrong sometimes,

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:12.840
but I'm just saying that I think that

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:14.080
we talked about this,

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:16.760
'cause there's this the one
couple that we don't think,

00:53:16.760 --> 00:53:18.550
the look on his face
when she says something,

00:53:18.550 --> 00:53:21.150
the look on her face, the physical,

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:23.450
just the way that they
seem to be connecting.

00:53:23.450 --> 00:53:25.850
And then there's another
couple that is immediately,

00:53:25.850 --> 00:53:27.570
you're like they got it,

00:53:27.570 --> 00:53:28.830
they're gonna be together a long time,

00:53:28.830 --> 00:53:30.390
I can see the way they interact.

00:53:30.390 --> 00:53:31.773
To me, it comes down to,

00:53:34.010 --> 00:53:35.300
and I don't know how long it takes,

00:53:35.300 --> 00:53:36.770
it varies from person to person.

00:53:36.770 --> 00:53:40.280
But if you can tell if somebody is like

00:53:40.280 --> 00:53:42.193
a genuine good person,

00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:47.520
but if I was trying to be
specific about my analysis,

00:53:47.520 --> 00:53:51.010
to me, something that would
be a telltale sign was like

00:53:51.010 --> 00:53:53.193
being able to quantify humility.

00:53:55.841 --> 00:54:00.660
If I'm listening to it on
a conversation and it's,

00:54:00.660 --> 00:54:04.200
you could tell that they don't seem like

00:54:04.200 --> 00:54:06.900
they're certain that they've
got it all figured out

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:10.990
or they're the type of person
that can say, they're sorry,

00:54:10.990 --> 00:54:15.890
you gotta be willing to be humble

00:54:15.890 --> 00:54:20.890
and admit your mistakes to
stay in vibrant relationship.

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:29.083
And I think when you've been
married, as long as we have,

00:54:32.367 --> 00:54:35.277
we really know our partners.

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:41.130
Like I know Christy, I was
gonna say inside and out,

00:54:41.130 --> 00:54:42.600
but then that would have been weird.

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:44.630
Right, well, but sort of true.

00:54:44.630 --> 00:54:47.573
It's totally true, but I
didn't mean it in that way.

00:54:48.690 --> 00:54:50.513
Get your mind off the magazines.

00:54:51.770 --> 00:54:54.153
You dirty minded a little boy.

00:54:55.257 --> 00:54:57.460
I am just here, I didn't say anything.

00:54:57.460 --> 00:55:01.110
I mean, we know each
other, we know everything,

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:03.840
we feel like we can predict
what the other person would say

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:05.950
or think or interact and what,

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:10.570
like we know what the other
person is capable of as friends.

00:55:10.570 --> 00:55:11.930
People think our friendship

00:55:11.930 --> 00:55:13.580
is so extraordinary for that reason,

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:17.930
but I feel like that's really
a requirement for marriage

00:55:17.930 --> 00:55:21.480
for long lasting marriage
is like, people are like,

00:55:21.480 --> 00:55:23.510
and I guess that's why people
say that about our friendship

00:55:23.510 --> 00:55:25.580
is like, because it's not a marriage

00:55:25.580 --> 00:55:28.260
and people don't invest
that much in friendship,

00:55:28.260 --> 00:55:30.507
but I think you have to
have that in a marriage,

00:55:30.507 --> 00:55:32.960
but it's such a high
standard to really know.

00:55:32.960 --> 00:55:37.230
But what I'm seeing in these
couples is like, literally,

00:55:37.230 --> 00:55:40.710
okay, they just got married.

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:42.250
But they're not, I mean-
Hold on.

00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:45.564
They just got married and then,

00:55:45.564 --> 00:55:49.210
after they get married,
the camera follows them

00:55:49.210 --> 00:55:51.010
as they walk back through everybody,

00:55:51.010 --> 00:55:52.550
we present to you, Mr. and Mrs. Johnson,

00:55:52.550 --> 00:55:56.250
they walk through everybody and
then they go to this little,

00:55:56.250 --> 00:55:57.370
they all get married at the same place

00:55:57.370 --> 00:55:58.270
someplace in Charlotte.

00:55:58.270 --> 00:56:00.330
And then they have their first date.

00:56:00.330 --> 00:56:01.940
No, they're just sitting
there talking to each other

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:03.760
and there's like a bottle of champagne.

00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:05.620
Like they do all the
couples have to do it.

00:56:05.620 --> 00:56:09.040
And it's just like, I looked at the way

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:13.110
that these two people
interacted with each other

00:56:14.004 --> 00:56:16.030
and the first moments of ever meeting.

00:56:16.030 --> 00:56:18.390
And I feel like I can
make a reliable prediction

00:56:18.390 --> 00:56:20.550
and that's not what the
psychologist was doing.

00:56:20.550 --> 00:56:24.100
So I don't think, I agree with
everything that you're saying

00:56:24.100 --> 00:56:26.770
that the key to like a continuing marriage

00:56:26.770 --> 00:56:30.160
is this learning about each
other is communicating,

00:56:30.160 --> 00:56:33.470
but I'm talking about compatibility

00:56:33.470 --> 00:56:35.890
that gives you the right runway to be able

00:56:35.890 --> 00:56:38.180
to establish those things
that you're talking about.

00:56:38.180 --> 00:56:40.840
I feel like it's something
that you can just sense

00:56:40.840 --> 00:56:43.000
in a sixth sense kind of way.

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:44.973
Now there are some people,
and this is the case-

00:56:44.973 --> 00:56:47.780
I think is unfair to put it
on the first time you meet,

00:56:47.780 --> 00:56:49.713
especially on television,
after you've gotten married,

00:56:49.713 --> 00:56:51.180
Because there's plenty of it.

00:56:51.180 --> 00:56:54.130
I'm just saying, I'm not
saying it's 100% right.

00:56:54.130 --> 00:56:55.900
I'm just saying that there is this,

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.920
you can sense compatibility
pretty quickly,

00:56:58.920 --> 00:57:02.380
but there's lots of people
who get married young

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:04.180
did not realize that they weren't.

00:57:04.180 --> 00:57:06.910
I mean, just because you're
the two people eligible people

00:57:06.910 --> 00:57:09.560
who get married in your
small town in the same year

00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:12.150
doesn't mean that you're
compatible, right?

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:13.620
It's statistics,

00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:15.960
you just weren't in the right
place at the right time.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:17.510
Then you start trying to figure out

00:57:17.510 --> 00:57:19.670
are you actually compatible?

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:23.610
And a lot of this, that's
where your commitment

00:57:23.610 --> 00:57:26.350
kind of goes into,
you've got compatibility

00:57:26.350 --> 00:57:27.640
and you've got commitment.

00:57:27.640 --> 00:57:29.890
And compatibility on its own

00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:32.800
cannot keep a marriage together forever.

00:57:32.800 --> 00:57:34.670
Commitment on its own can,

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:36.580
there are people who hate each other,

00:57:36.580 --> 00:57:38.590
but because they don't believe in divorce,

00:57:38.590 --> 00:57:40.600
they're just gonna be together forever.

00:57:40.600 --> 00:57:43.570
I think that the beautiful
marriages are the ones.

00:57:43.570 --> 00:57:46.027
And once there is a compatibility

00:57:46.027 --> 00:57:47.530
and that compatibility can grow

00:57:47.530 --> 00:57:50.850
because you change over time,

00:57:50.850 --> 00:57:52.820
that's coupled with a commitment

00:57:52.820 --> 00:57:54.770
that then there are these
beautiful moments of like,

00:57:54.770 --> 00:57:56.130
we actually love each other,

00:57:56.130 --> 00:57:57.780
have a great time with each other,

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:00.340
but that's not always gonna
happen because I'm a human,

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:02.430
you're a human, I'm gonna be an asshole,

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:04.040
you're gonna be an asshole.

00:58:04.040 --> 00:58:06.993
And in those moments to get
through the asshole moments.

00:58:08.260 --> 00:58:09.780
You got to stay committed.

00:58:09.780 --> 00:58:13.200
That's when you gotta have
that commitment in place.

00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:15.650
And the only thing I'm
saying is how crazy it was

00:58:15.650 --> 00:58:16.483
to look at these couples

00:58:16.483 --> 00:58:18.520
and make a decision about compatibility.

00:58:18.520 --> 00:58:21.620
I would add it as a commitment
to a couple of things.

00:58:21.620 --> 00:58:25.550
It's not just a commitment
to staying together.

00:58:25.550 --> 00:58:29.033
I think it's a commitment to being,

00:58:30.153 --> 00:58:33.350
it's the reason why there are vows.

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:35.700
It's like you commit to the certain things

00:58:35.700 --> 00:58:36.760
that you're gonna do.

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:39.830
You serve and love the other person

00:58:39.830 --> 00:58:42.750
place their needs before your needs

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:45.080
in sickness and in health.

00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:49.873
Like that sickness thing,
that can get real tough.

00:58:53.157 --> 00:58:56.800
And that's a huge commitment
and it's a blind commitment

00:58:56.800 --> 00:58:58.970
and for most people who haven't

00:58:59.830 --> 00:59:01.830
care for someone who's really been sick.

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:07.810
So I think it's a
commitment to those things

00:59:07.810 --> 00:59:12.810
that you're promising that
you're gonna make it work.

00:59:12.810 --> 00:59:17.390
I'm committed to dancing with you.

00:59:17.390 --> 00:59:19.590
Sometimes the music is gonna suck.

00:59:19.590 --> 00:59:22.333
Sometimes our legs are gonna be broken,

00:59:26.120 --> 00:59:30.537
but we're committed to
dancing together through life.

00:59:32.110 --> 00:59:33.840
And that's something that

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:38.500
it requires constant
cooperation and fidelity.

00:59:38.500 --> 00:59:40.950
Well, and I think that the
thing that is difficult

00:59:40.950 --> 00:59:43.863
for a lot of couples,

00:59:48.060 --> 00:59:49.880
and this should be incorporated

00:59:49.880 --> 00:59:52.170
into the more traditional vows if it isn't

00:59:54.180 --> 00:59:56.140
a commitment to communicate.

00:59:56.140 --> 00:59:58.577
And I think that people
have a difficult time

00:59:58.577 --> 01:00:00.160
and this can be personality based,

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:03.560
but you know how sometimes
when you're having

01:00:03.560 --> 01:00:06.420
a difficult moment with
someone who's a friend,

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:08.703
any relationship.

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:13.440
And sometimes what you have to do

01:00:13.440 --> 01:00:16.130
is you have to have
that meta conversation.

01:00:16.130 --> 01:00:20.310
You have to talk about the way
that you talk to each other.

01:00:20.310 --> 01:00:22.300
You have to be like, you have to sit,

01:00:22.300 --> 01:00:26.190
some couples get to a place where,

01:00:26.190 --> 01:00:27.720
Oh, we're not really communicating,

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:29.760
we're not connecting anymore.

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:32.930
And we have lost the ability to talk about

01:00:32.930 --> 01:00:35.170
the fact that we're
not connecting anymore.

01:00:35.170 --> 01:00:38.150
And I think that that happens
in so many relationships,

01:00:38.150 --> 01:00:40.760
it's like, if you stop talking about

01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:44.390
the nature of your connection
is gonna be very difficult

01:00:44.390 --> 01:00:49.390
to get the connection back
without addressing that directly.

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:53.370
If you're just focused
on the superficial stuff,

01:00:53.370 --> 01:00:57.130
the specifics of the current
argument that you're in,

01:00:57.130 --> 01:00:59.860
but you haven't gotten to the,

01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:02.600
how do you actually connect
and how you communicate

01:01:02.600 --> 01:01:05.500
solving this particular problem
is not gonna be helpful.

01:01:05.500 --> 01:01:09.510
It's just gonna just kick
the can down the road,

01:01:09.510 --> 01:01:11.780
and I think that's what
you were getting at earlier

01:01:11.780 --> 01:01:14.270
with the marriage counseling is like,

01:01:14.270 --> 01:01:17.453
it helps you having a
third party a lot of times,

01:01:18.590 --> 01:01:21.760
Oh, we can have the uncomfortable
conversation that we,

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:24.713
for some reason can't have
with just the two of us.

01:01:25.620 --> 01:01:28.570
You break through to that,
plane, its a different plane.

01:01:28.570 --> 01:01:29.750
Not just premarital,

01:01:29.750 --> 01:01:34.750
but like marriage therapy is very good,

01:01:37.180 --> 01:01:39.957
sometimes you just have to
bring in a third person.

01:01:39.957 --> 01:01:41.830
And my therapist is my wife's therapist,

01:01:41.830 --> 01:01:46.830
which is not necessarily
advisable across the board,

01:01:47.140 --> 01:01:52.050
but in our situation,
because a lot of the times

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:54.750
what we're doing is we're
talking about each other

01:01:54.750 --> 01:01:58.850
is like, well, my therapist has
the other side of the story.

01:01:58.850 --> 01:02:02.350
And then occasionally, if
there's like an acute issue,

01:02:02.350 --> 01:02:07.350
we'll both go in together,
and that has been helpful.

01:02:07.750 --> 01:02:11.910
I think, knowing that most relationships,

01:02:11.910 --> 01:02:15.710
I'm not gonna say all, there's
this entropy, right, of it

01:02:15.710 --> 01:02:18.870
okay, this isn't gonna last forever

01:02:18.870 --> 01:02:21.620
because we're both imperfect people

01:02:21.620 --> 01:02:23.720
that have are prone to selfishness.

01:02:23.720 --> 01:02:27.470
And we're not the same person,

01:02:27.470 --> 01:02:31.330
and there's so many
variables, you never know

01:02:31.330 --> 01:02:34.340
what could be thrown at us as a couple.

01:02:34.340 --> 01:02:38.760
So then it's just having
this mentality that okay,

01:02:38.760 --> 01:02:43.310
if that's the tendency,
then we have to be vigilant

01:02:43.310 --> 01:02:46.590
about the threats to our relationship

01:02:47.620 --> 01:02:51.010
from internal and external
and just know that,

01:02:51.010 --> 01:02:54.760
okay, we're gonna identify,

01:02:54.760 --> 01:02:57.930
and we're gonna face
these threats head on.

01:02:57.930 --> 01:02:59.730
And not just because we know

01:02:59.730 --> 01:03:03.540
that that's what's most likely to happen

01:03:03.540 --> 01:03:06.630
is that something is
gonna sneak up on you.

01:03:06.630 --> 01:03:08.600
And then a few months, you're like,

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:11.466
okay, there's a wall that's built up.

01:03:11.466 --> 01:03:16.190
This is a threat to our marriage longterm.

01:03:16.190 --> 01:03:21.183
It's not just lean to a
bad week or a bad month.

01:03:22.573 --> 01:03:26.990
I felt like I had this
very heightened commitment,

01:03:26.990 --> 01:03:29.350
Christy's parents are still together,

01:03:29.350 --> 01:03:32.663
my parents were in my
experience were never together.

01:03:37.508 --> 01:03:39.425
And so I had this mentality that like,

01:03:39.425 --> 01:03:42.223
I was gonna stay committed,

01:03:43.850 --> 01:03:45.570
divorce was never an option

01:03:49.820 --> 01:03:53.053
I haven't reassessed
all of that mentality.

01:03:54.440 --> 01:03:56.347
Maybe there's some
negative aspects to that.

01:03:56.347 --> 01:03:59.520
And I think you can lean
on that and it's like,

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:01.820
it minimizes the amount
of work that goes into it.

01:04:01.820 --> 01:04:04.320
You just can't say, well,
divorce is not an option.

01:04:04.320 --> 01:04:07.170
Like we've already talked
about, that's not how you dance

01:04:08.230 --> 01:04:10.617
just by saying, well, I'm gonna stand here

01:04:10.617 --> 01:04:14.090
and I'm gonna be here, that's not dancing.

01:04:14.090 --> 01:04:17.756
And I thought statistically,
you hear this thrown around,

01:04:17.756 --> 01:04:19.660
I don't know if it's true,

01:04:19.660 --> 01:04:22.600
I don't know what the
latest statistics are but

01:04:22.600 --> 01:04:24.940
I always heard when I was in the church

01:04:24.940 --> 01:04:27.640
that the divorce rate inside and outside

01:04:27.640 --> 01:04:29.623
of the church is about the same.

01:04:30.632 --> 01:04:32.760
I always found that hard to believe,

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:34.670
just because I didn't see as much divorce

01:04:34.670 --> 01:04:36.230
in the circles that I was in,

01:04:36.230 --> 01:04:38.500
because it felt like
people were more committed

01:04:38.500 --> 01:04:43.130
because they did have this sort
of ideological underpinning

01:04:43.130 --> 01:04:47.620
that included divorce is not an option.

01:04:47.620 --> 01:04:51.820
And I do think that there
is a dark side to that

01:04:51.820 --> 01:04:54.430
because a lot of times it ends up being,

01:04:54.430 --> 01:04:56.770
and I've witnessed this not with friends,

01:04:56.770 --> 01:04:58.450
but just with people
that we knew in church,

01:04:58.450 --> 01:05:01.900
where there would be like this
person is being emotionally,

01:05:01.900 --> 01:05:03.693
maybe even physically abused.

01:05:04.576 --> 01:05:07.160
And yet they're staying in this marriage

01:05:07.160 --> 01:05:09.350
because they have this
ideological commitment

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:11.847
and that's for them to figure out.

01:05:11.847 --> 01:05:14.143
Or for the kids or for other reasons,

01:05:16.670 --> 01:05:20.230
but as it applies to us, I think that,

01:05:20.230 --> 01:05:22.570
'cause my parents are still together.

01:05:22.570 --> 01:05:24.370
Jessie's parents are still together,

01:05:25.460 --> 01:05:27.150
so I didn't have that same sort of like,

01:05:27.150 --> 01:05:29.290
well, I'm gonna be different.

01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:32.210
But I brought the same
mentality that you did

01:05:32.210 --> 01:05:34.480
to the marriage is like-

01:05:34.480 --> 01:05:36.673
It is nice to have it off the table,

01:05:37.508 --> 01:05:41.170
where you agree from the beginning,

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:43.610
from before the beginning
of being married,

01:05:43.610 --> 01:05:48.507
that like divorce under
normal circumstances

01:05:50.240 --> 01:05:51.760
is not an option,

01:05:51.760 --> 01:05:55.230
we're not gonna use it as
a relational escape patch.

01:05:55.230 --> 01:05:57.180
It's a last resort.

01:05:57.180 --> 01:05:59.990
And I do think that there
is again, and this is not-

01:05:59.990 --> 01:06:01.750
When it comes to relational that I guess.

01:06:01.750 --> 01:06:05.803
I'm not judging anybody and
I'm not prescribing anything.

01:06:08.319 --> 01:06:10.480
I would have a difficult time,

01:06:10.480 --> 01:06:12.390
obviously kids change the picture

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:17.110
because you've got some people
who are gonna be affected,

01:06:17.110 --> 01:06:19.150
especially if they're kids
that you're currently raising

01:06:19.150 --> 01:06:20.450
they're in the house.

01:06:20.450 --> 01:06:22.300
But if there's a couple that again,

01:06:22.300 --> 01:06:23.670
just like marriage at this show,

01:06:23.670 --> 01:06:26.800
which this has become an ad
for this show unintentionally,

01:06:26.800 --> 01:06:29.280
but like, okay, if you
believe that getting married

01:06:29.280 --> 01:06:31.960
and getting divorced on a
television show is wrong,

01:06:31.960 --> 01:06:33.540
then I can't argue with that.

01:06:33.540 --> 01:06:35.400
I'm not gonna change your mind about that.

01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:39.300
But if you think that
these people came into this

01:06:39.300 --> 01:06:42.360
on their own volition, then
they together made the decision.

01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:43.300
They weren't compatible,

01:06:43.300 --> 01:06:45.500
they haven't brought
any kids into this yet.

01:06:46.489 --> 01:06:47.640
And then they make a decision

01:06:47.640 --> 01:06:51.470
to mutually separate and get divorced.

01:06:51.470 --> 01:06:52.570
Then I'm not gonna be like,

01:06:52.570 --> 01:06:54.070
well, you have dishonored

01:06:55.160 --> 01:06:57.200
the institution of marriage by doing that.

01:06:57.200 --> 01:07:00.540
And I'm also gonna say
that if it's somebody

01:07:02.190 --> 01:07:05.380
everyone has their own reasons for it.

01:07:05.380 --> 01:07:10.380
But I think that it's the
idea that this committee,

01:07:11.470 --> 01:07:13.550
this is like this, isn't
just a serious thing.

01:07:13.550 --> 01:07:14.700
This is a commitment

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:17.130
that through all these
potential circumstances

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:18.470
that we're gonna stay together.

01:07:18.470 --> 01:07:20.340
Because if we don't think like that,

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:22.410
we're gonna see a way out.

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:26.900
And then in your lowest moments,
you might go for the door

01:07:26.900 --> 01:07:28.640
if you've installed the door.

01:07:28.640 --> 01:07:30.410
But if you've never installed the door,

01:07:30.410 --> 01:07:33.180
you're not gonna leave, but you know what?

01:07:33.180 --> 01:07:35.780
You could decide at a certain
point in your relationship

01:07:35.780 --> 01:07:39.170
together, hey, let's install the door.

01:07:39.170 --> 01:07:41.630
If you come to that
conclusion as a couple.

01:07:41.630 --> 01:07:44.140
And you're like, I think that
we need to seriously consider

01:07:44.140 --> 01:07:46.110
that maybe we're not supposed

01:07:46.110 --> 01:07:48.020
to be in relationship with each other.

01:07:48.020 --> 01:07:50.690
There was a time in my life

01:07:50.690 --> 01:07:52.600
when I subscribed to an
evangelical worldview

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:55.120
where I would be like,
that's the devil talking.

01:07:55.120 --> 01:07:57.300
You can't go there, 'cause that's

01:07:57.300 --> 01:07:59.020
now you're talking about
building the escape hatch

01:07:59.020 --> 01:08:00.630
and you're gonna do it.

01:08:00.630 --> 01:08:02.940
And my perspective on that has changed.

01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:06.400
It's like, it's not my
role to judge other couples

01:08:06.400 --> 01:08:07.960
and how they go about that

01:08:09.070 --> 01:08:11.550
but what I can say from
speaking from own experiences

01:08:11.550 --> 01:08:14.340
is that not building the escape patch,

01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:18.240
not putting the door in
has been one of the reasons

01:08:18.240 --> 01:08:20.780
I can point to for being
married for 19 years

01:08:23.320 --> 01:08:24.750
For me.

01:08:24.750 --> 01:08:25.943
And for me too.

01:08:30.260 --> 01:08:33.040
I'm sure that there's
people in our position

01:08:33.040 --> 01:08:34.530
who were saying similar things

01:08:34.530 --> 01:08:36.310
and then down the road, they get divorced.

01:08:36.310 --> 01:08:37.590
Of course.

01:08:37.590 --> 01:08:40.030
Even that couple that I was
making all these assumptions

01:08:40.030 --> 01:08:41.630
about because they're public

01:08:41.630 --> 01:08:43.430
and they're saying they're friends

01:08:45.110 --> 01:08:48.240
that's just me bringing
my assumptions to it,

01:08:48.240 --> 01:08:50.350
I don't even know their names.

01:08:50.350 --> 01:08:52.123
What are the names let's say them?

01:08:53.571 --> 01:08:54.580
It does get complicated

01:08:54.580 --> 01:08:59.580
and I do wanna make a commitment
to not judge other people.

01:09:01.570 --> 01:09:02.770
I mean, cause it's like,

01:09:05.020 --> 01:09:07.220
who have been, or are
going through a divorce.

01:09:07.220 --> 01:09:10.630
I think there's clear because
what I hear you and me

01:09:10.630 --> 01:09:15.070
saying is that if what you
have collectively decided to do

01:09:15.070 --> 01:09:19.180
is this, then there are certain
decisions that you can make

01:09:19.180 --> 01:09:22.990
to help make that
reality become a reality.

01:09:22.990 --> 01:09:24.893
That idea become a reality.

01:09:26.540 --> 01:09:27.870
What you've made together decide

01:09:27.870 --> 01:09:29.830
that you want a different reality.

01:09:29.830 --> 01:09:33.760
Well, 'cause everything
that we said seems to imply

01:09:34.888 --> 01:09:37.990
that the high road is to stay-

01:09:37.990 --> 01:09:38.823
Married forever.

01:09:38.823 --> 01:09:39.750
Married forever.

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:41.320
And I used to think that,

01:09:41.320 --> 01:09:43.080
and there is something beautiful about it.

01:09:43.080 --> 01:09:45.590
I'm not gonna argue with that.

01:09:45.590 --> 01:09:48.410
And I do think there is
something beautiful about

01:09:48.410 --> 01:09:51.490
like overcoming adversity and like just

01:09:51.490 --> 01:09:54.780
the nature of commitment,
but you know what,

01:09:54.780 --> 01:09:57.870
there's plenty of examples of people

01:09:57.870 --> 01:09:59.517
who were in a difficult marriage

01:09:59.517 --> 01:10:00.990
and not just necessarily one

01:10:00.990 --> 01:10:03.550
that they needed to get out
of for like safety reasons,

01:10:03.550 --> 01:10:06.070
but just people who
mutually discovered that

01:10:06.070 --> 01:10:07.970
they wanted something different.

01:10:07.970 --> 01:10:12.970
Now there each and other
beautiful, committed relationships.

01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:15.010
That's a reality as well.

01:10:15.010 --> 01:10:17.330
And there was a time in my life.

01:10:17.330 --> 01:10:19.790
Again, if you subscribe to

01:10:19.790 --> 01:10:23.160
sort of a conservative,
evangelical worldview,

01:10:23.160 --> 01:10:25.300
then if you really read

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:27.410
certain parts of the
Bible in a certain way,

01:10:27.410 --> 01:10:29.120
then those marriages after divorce

01:10:29.120 --> 01:10:31.630
are illegitimate marriages.

01:10:31.630 --> 01:10:33.910
And I'm glad that I don't
have to think that anymore

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:38.690
because the observational,
my experience is that

01:10:38.690 --> 01:10:41.240
no actually some of those
marriages are better

01:10:41.240 --> 01:10:43.590
and healthier and better for these people

01:10:43.590 --> 01:10:46.010
and better for the world
than the previous marriage.

01:10:46.010 --> 01:10:47.890
And I'm not gonna cancel that marriage

01:10:47.890 --> 01:10:50.110
because I got some ideological commitment.

01:10:50.110 --> 01:10:52.560
How do you think my
mom felt over the years

01:10:52.560 --> 01:10:55.650
when like culturally, we
were in an environment

01:10:55.650 --> 01:11:00.650
where it was like, you
can't, I mean, you failed.

01:11:01.830 --> 01:11:03.110
Something is wrong with you.

01:11:03.110 --> 01:11:04.660
Twice.

01:11:04.660 --> 01:11:05.960
You're in your third marriage now.

01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:09.570
It's like, you failed twice.

01:11:09.570 --> 01:11:12.400
And that's not what happened.

01:11:12.400 --> 01:11:13.233
And in many places,

01:11:13.233 --> 01:11:15.020
you're not qualified to be in leadership.

01:11:15.020 --> 01:11:16.680
If you're in a relationship like that.

01:11:16.680 --> 01:11:19.530
Like you've kind of like,
you've got this mark on you

01:11:19.530 --> 01:11:20.909
and you can never recover.

01:11:20.909 --> 01:11:23.710
I think we've said
everything through the filter

01:11:25.242 --> 01:11:30.242
through the lens of, we love
our wives and they love us.

01:11:32.070 --> 01:11:37.070
And that's, I wanna be
with Christy until I die.

01:11:39.250 --> 01:11:40.820
Cause she'll already be dead, no

01:11:40.820 --> 01:11:42.091
or until she dies.

01:11:42.091 --> 01:11:45.140
I mean, I just wanna cover the
basis until one of us dies.

01:11:45.140 --> 01:11:47.410
It's called till death do us part.

01:11:47.410 --> 01:11:49.430
Yeah, I don't wanna start, like-

01:11:49.430 --> 01:11:51.370
They took care of that a long time.

01:11:51.370 --> 01:11:52.523
Who dies first?

01:11:54.440 --> 01:11:55.840
It's not until I, or you die.

01:11:55.840 --> 01:11:57.253
It's till death do us part.

01:11:58.610 --> 01:12:00.290
You don't have to think
about who dies first.

01:12:00.290 --> 01:12:01.123
You just say that.

01:12:01.123 --> 01:12:03.190
'Cause you don't wanna
have to unravel that.

01:12:03.190 --> 01:12:04.550
I told Jessie she's younger than me,

01:12:04.550 --> 01:12:06.530
I'm tall, I'm gonna die first.

01:12:06.530 --> 01:12:09.090
I mean, I'm just going with the stats,

01:12:09.090 --> 01:12:11.865
tall people don't live that long, sorry.

01:12:11.865 --> 01:12:13.940
She gets very upset when
I start talking like that.

01:12:13.940 --> 01:12:15.760
So commitment looks different for her

01:12:15.760 --> 01:12:16.593
than it does for you.

01:12:16.593 --> 01:12:19.460
Yeah, I'm like, listen, if things go as

01:12:19.460 --> 01:12:22.200
according to just like nature,

01:12:22.200 --> 01:12:24.960
you're gonna have at least a few years

01:12:24.960 --> 01:12:27.233
to just find a new man.

01:12:29.670 --> 01:12:31.520
How does that feel?

01:12:31.520 --> 01:12:32.720
I mean, you're joking now but-

01:12:32.720 --> 01:12:35.793
She hates when I talk, I love
to joke with her about that.

01:12:35.793 --> 01:12:37.190
Because she says things like,

01:12:37.190 --> 01:12:40.110
well, if I died, how long would
it take you to get married?

01:12:40.110 --> 01:12:41.220
And?

01:12:41.220 --> 01:12:42.930
And then I say things like,

01:12:42.930 --> 01:12:45.000
I don't know if I would get married

01:12:45.000 --> 01:12:46.810
and then she gets really mad.

01:12:46.810 --> 01:12:48.060
Oh really?

01:12:48.060 --> 01:12:50.290
Cause she wants you to get married.

01:12:50.290 --> 01:12:51.820
She don't want you to be what?

01:12:51.820 --> 01:12:53.725
a Philander or Playboy or something?

01:12:53.725 --> 01:12:54.558
I don't know, and I was like,

01:12:54.558 --> 01:12:56.880
I just haven't thought
about it, cross that bridge

01:12:56.880 --> 01:12:59.320
if we get there, you know what I'm saying?

01:12:59.320 --> 01:13:01.760
But like, I don't know.

01:13:01.760 --> 01:13:02.910
I'm a different person.

01:13:04.750 --> 01:13:06.820
If you're honest though, I
think what you would say,

01:13:06.820 --> 01:13:07.720
I know what you would say.

01:13:07.720 --> 01:13:10.070
You'd say, well, I've
been watching this show.

01:13:10.070 --> 01:13:11.470
I'm gonna go on this show.

01:13:12.777 --> 01:13:14.790
That season three of
"Married at First Sight"

01:13:14.790 --> 01:13:16.903
is everyone is a widower or a widow.

01:13:18.250 --> 01:13:20.963
I think I just gave you all a good idea.

01:13:22.780 --> 01:13:24.280
That's gonna get real heavy.

01:13:27.109 --> 01:13:28.563
Talk about crying while you watch it.

01:13:28.563 --> 01:13:31.250
And now you gotta hear all the stories

01:13:31.250 --> 01:13:33.960
of how their spouse died,
but that's a bad idea.

01:13:33.960 --> 01:13:36.848
I mean, but as a producer,
they have to think about it.

01:13:36.848 --> 01:13:38.270
They've been talking about it.

01:13:38.270 --> 01:13:41.160
Oh yeah, hopefully a producer
of "Married at First Sight"

01:13:41.160 --> 01:13:42.570
is listening in.

01:13:42.570 --> 01:13:44.383
So we ended up just talking about-

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:46.700
Marriage.

01:13:46.700 --> 01:13:47.650
We talked about marriage,

01:13:47.650 --> 01:13:49.063
but we really just talking
about our marriages.

01:13:49.063 --> 01:13:51.080
I mean, I thought about
those four marriages

01:13:51.080 --> 01:13:52.490
on that television show as well.

01:13:52.490 --> 01:13:54.820
I mean, I didn't really
get into specifics.

01:13:54.820 --> 01:13:57.300
So is that your recommendation this week?

01:13:57.300 --> 01:13:59.688
What's the name of the television show?

01:13:59.688 --> 01:14:02.140
Arranged and arranged.

01:14:02.140 --> 01:14:04.230
I'm gonna give another
recommendation on top of that.

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:05.260
What is the name of the show?

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:07.990
It's "Married at First
Sight", it's on Netflix.

01:14:07.990 --> 01:14:09.700
I can only vouch for
the most recent season.

01:14:09.700 --> 01:14:10.740
I don't know what season that is.

01:14:10.740 --> 01:14:13.400
It might be season two,
I'm only four episodes in,

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:15.474
but I do have another wreck

01:14:15.474 --> 01:14:17.460
that goes back to what we
were talking about before,

01:14:17.460 --> 01:14:18.900
which is some of the
things that we continue

01:14:18.900 --> 01:14:21.400
to be dealing with as a society

01:14:22.280 --> 01:14:24.330
for the betterment of our society.

01:14:24.330 --> 01:14:25.330
And it's a book that

01:14:28.650 --> 01:14:30.300
I made it a recommendation in my letter

01:14:30.300 --> 01:14:31.330
to a white man that I wrote,

01:14:31.330 --> 01:14:32.587
but I'm gonna talk about
it specifically here.

01:14:32.587 --> 01:14:35.937
And that is, "I'm Still
Here: Black Dignity

01:14:35.937 --> 01:14:39.233
"In A World Made For Whiteness"
by Austin, Channing Brown.

01:14:40.650 --> 01:14:45.140
And this book, I'm just gonna
read the description here

01:14:45.140 --> 01:14:47.800
that was made by whoever
wrote this particular review,

01:14:47.800 --> 01:14:51.667
the Hello Sunshine Book
Pick, a Reese's Book Club.

01:14:51.667 --> 01:14:53.897
"From a leading voice on racial justice

01:14:53.897 --> 01:14:56.777
"and eye opening account of
growing up black Christian

01:14:56.777 --> 01:14:59.657
"and female that exposes
how white America,

01:14:59.657 --> 01:15:02.387
"white America is a love
affair with diversity.

01:15:02.387 --> 01:15:06.160
"So often falls short of its ideals."

01:15:06.160 --> 01:15:08.470
And I know that you
started this book as well,

01:15:08.470 --> 01:15:12.010
but the general idea of the book

01:15:12.010 --> 01:15:14.060
is exactly what was just described there.

01:15:15.834 --> 01:15:17.240
That we can come to this place

01:15:17.240 --> 01:15:18.580
and this is something that is happening

01:15:18.580 --> 01:15:21.630
in the midst of the movement right now,

01:15:21.630 --> 01:15:23.900
where a bunch of white people can be like,

01:15:23.900 --> 01:15:26.460
yes, we value diversity,

01:15:26.460 --> 01:15:30.300
and we believe that is something
that should be represented

01:15:31.840 --> 01:15:34.950
in our circles of
friends, in our businesses

01:15:34.950 --> 01:15:38.990
in society, in our leadership
and our legislature.

01:15:38.990 --> 01:15:40.260
It's the right thing to say.

01:15:40.260 --> 01:15:43.200
And it's an easy thing to say right now,

01:15:43.200 --> 01:15:44.800
One, her point is-

01:15:44.800 --> 01:15:46.260
Challenging to enact.

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:48.750
She tells from a very
personal perspective,

01:15:48.750 --> 01:15:50.330
what it has been like

01:15:50.330 --> 01:15:53.920
to be in white spaces as a black woman

01:15:53.920 --> 01:15:57.040
in spaces that said that
this is what they valued.

01:15:57.040 --> 01:15:59.680
And maybe even thought that
this is what they valued,

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:03.253
but didn't understand
what that actually meant.

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:08.390
It's incredibly challenging,
it's incredibly eyeopening.

01:16:08.390 --> 01:16:09.760
I think it actually, I don't know

01:16:09.760 --> 01:16:11.740
if it wasn't New York times
bestseller from the beginning

01:16:11.740 --> 01:16:14.170
or if it has become a
New York times bestseller

01:16:14.170 --> 01:16:15.560
in the midst of this movement,

01:16:15.560 --> 01:16:17.150
but it is a New York times bestseller

01:16:17.150 --> 01:16:19.030
'cause it's two years old,

01:16:19.030 --> 01:16:21.740
but it is really sort of experiencing

01:16:21.740 --> 01:16:25.800
this new wave of interest
because it speaks so directly

01:16:25.800 --> 01:16:28.170
to the movement that
is happening right now.

01:16:28.170 --> 01:16:29.810
And I think more importantly,

01:16:29.810 --> 01:16:33.740
or even more importantly for a
couple of white dudes like us

01:16:33.740 --> 01:16:37.100
is asking the question of
what does moving beyond

01:16:37.100 --> 01:16:42.100
valuing diversity to making diversity

01:16:42.290 --> 01:16:46.663
an inclusivity and sort
of deconstructing this,

01:16:47.752 --> 01:16:50.130
white is the norm mentality

01:16:50.130 --> 01:16:52.350
that a lot of us have
without even realizing it.

01:16:52.350 --> 01:16:53.860
What does it take to break that down?

01:16:53.860 --> 01:16:55.760
What does it take to move beyond that?

01:16:55.760 --> 01:17:00.760
Because that's the real
work that needs to happen?

01:17:00.870 --> 01:17:03.798
It's not just recognizing
the racial injustice

01:17:03.798 --> 01:17:06.170
is doing something about it
and having a real change.

01:17:06.170 --> 01:17:10.230
So "I'm Still Here" by
Austin Channing Brown,

01:17:10.230 --> 01:17:12.350
and it is a pretty easy read.

01:17:12.350 --> 01:17:15.830
So no excuses for not
checking this one out,

01:17:15.830 --> 01:17:17.410
wherever you check out books,

01:17:17.410 --> 01:17:18.910
you could just buy the book as well,

01:17:18.910 --> 01:17:20.160
don't just check it out.

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:22.720
All right, we'll speak
at you again next week.

